#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:1
msgid "The `#[doc]` attribute"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:3
msgid ""
"The `#[doc]` attribute lets you control various aspects of how `rustdoc` "
"does its job."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:6
msgid ""
"The most basic function of `#[doc]` is to handle the actual documentation "
"text. That is, `///` is syntax sugar for `#[doc]` (as is `//!` for `#!"
"[doc]`). This means that these two are the same:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:11
msgid "/// This is a doc comment.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:12
msgid "\" This is a doc comment.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:16
msgid ""
"(Note the leading space and the raw string literal in the attribute version.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:18
msgid ""
"In most cases, `///` is easier to use than `#[doc]`. One case where the "
"latter is easier is when generating documentation in macros; the `collapse-"
"docs` pass will combine multiple `#[doc]` attributes into a single doc "
"comment, letting you generate code like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:23
msgid "\"This is\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:24
msgid "\" a \""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:25
msgid "\"doc comment\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:29
msgid "Which can feel more flexible. Note that this would generate this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:32
msgid "\"This is\\n a \\ndoc comment\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:36
msgid ""
"but given that docs are rendered via Markdown, it will remove these newlines."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:38
msgid "Another use case is for including external files as documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:41
msgid "\"../../README.md\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:45
msgid ""
"The `doc` attribute has more options though! These don't involve the text of "
"the output, but instead, various aspects of the presentation of the output. "
"We've split them into two kinds below: attributes that are useful at the "
"crate level, and ones that are useful at the item level."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:50
msgid "At the crate level"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:52
msgid "These options control how the docs look at a crate level."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:54
msgid "`html_favicon_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:56
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute lets you control the favicon of your docs."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:59
msgid "\"https://example.com/favicon.ico\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:62
msgid ""
"This will put `<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"{}\">` into your docs, where the "
"string for the attribute goes into the `{}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:65
msgid "If you don't use this attribute, a default favicon will be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:67
msgid "`html_logo_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:69
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute lets you control the logo in the upper left "
"hand side of the docs."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:73
msgid "\"https://example.com/logo.jpg\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:76
msgid ""
"This will put `<a href='../index.html'><img src='{}' alt='logo' "
"width='100'></a>` into your docs, where the string for the attribute goes "
"into the `{}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:79
msgid "If you don't use this attribute, there will be no logo."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:81
msgid "`html_playground_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:83
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute lets you control where the \"run\" buttons "
"on your documentation examples make requests to."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:87
msgid "\"https://playground.example.com/\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:90
msgid ""
"Now, when you press \"run\", the button will make a request to this domain. "
"The request URL will contain 3 query parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:92
msgid "`code` for the code in the documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:93
msgid ""
"`version` for the Rust channel, e.g. nightly, which is decided by whether "
"`code` contain unstable features"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:94
msgid "`edition` for the Rust edition, e.g. 2024"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:96
msgid "If you don't use this attribute, there will be no run buttons."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:98
msgid "`issue_tracker_base_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:100
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute is mostly only useful for the standard "
"library; When a feature is unstable, an issue number for tracking the "
"feature must be given. `rustdoc` uses this number, plus the base URL given "
"here, to link to the tracking issue."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:106
msgid "\"https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:109
msgid "`html_root_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:111
msgid ""
"The `#[doc(html_root_url = \"…\")]` attribute value indicates the URL for "
"generating links to external crates. When rustdoc needs to generate a link "
"to an item in an external crate, it will first check if the extern crate has "
"been documented locally on-disk, and if so link directly to it. Failing "
"that, it will use the URL given by the `--extern-html-root-url` command-line "
"flag if available. If that is not available, then it will use the "
"`html_root_url` value in the extern crate if it is available. If that is not "
"available, then the extern items will not be linked."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:121
msgid "\"https://docs.rs/serde/1.0\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:124
msgid "`html_no_source`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:126
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc` will include the source code of your program, with "
"links to it in the docs. But if you include this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:133
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:144
msgid "it will not."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:135
msgid "`test(no_crate_inject)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:137
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc` will automatically add a line with `extern crate "
"my_crate;` into each doctest. But if you include this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:146
msgid "At the item level"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:148
msgid ""
"These forms of the `#[doc]` attribute are used on individual items, to "
"control how they are documented."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:151
msgid "`inline` and `no_inline`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:153
msgid "<span id=\"docno_inlinedocinline\"></span>"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:155
msgid ""
"These attributes are used on `use` statements, and control where the "
"documentation shows up. For example, consider this Rust code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:160
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:188
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:205
msgid "/// bar docs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:163
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:191
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:208
msgid "/// the docs for Bar\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:169
msgid ""
"The documentation will generate a \"Re-exports\" section, and say `pub use "
"bar::Bar;`, where `Bar` is a link to its page."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:172
msgid "If we change the `use` line like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:181
msgid ""
"Instead, `Bar` will appear in a `Structs` section, just like `Bar` was "
"defined at the top level, rather than `pub use`'d."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:184
msgid "Let's change our original example, by making `bar` private:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:197
msgid ""
"Here, because `bar` is not public, `bar` wouldn't have its own page, so "
"there's nowhere to link to. `rustdoc` will inline these definitions, and so "
"we end up in the same case as the `#[doc(inline)]` above; `Bar` is in a "
"`Structs` section, as if it were defined at the top level. If we add the "
"`no_inline` form of the attribute:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:214
msgid ""
"Now we'll have a `Re-exports` line, and `Bar` will not link to anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:216
msgid ""
"One special case: In Rust 2018 and later, if you `pub use` one of your "
"dependencies, `rustdoc` will not eagerly inline it as a module unless you "
"add `#[doc(inline)]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:219
msgid ""
"If you want to know more about inlining rules, take a look at the [`re-"
"exports` chapter](./re-exports.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:222
msgid "`hidden`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:224
msgid "<span id=\"dochidden\"></span>"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:226
msgid ""
"Any item annotated with `#[doc(hidden)]` will not appear in the "
"documentation, unless the [`--document-hidden-items`](../unstable-features."
"md#document-hidden-items) flag is used."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:229
msgid ""
"You can find more information in the [`re-exports` chapter](./re-exports.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:231
msgid "`alias`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:233
msgid "This attribute adds an alias in the search index."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:235
msgid "Let's take an example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:238
msgid "\"TheAlias\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:242
msgid ""
"So now, if you enter \"TheAlias\" in the search, it'll display `SomeType`. "
"Of course, if you enter `SomeType` it'll return `SomeType` as expected!"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:245
msgid "FFI example"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:247
msgid ""
"This doc attribute is especially useful when writing bindings for a C "
"library. For example, let's say we have a C function that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:254
msgid ""
"It takes a pointer to an `Obj` type and returns an integer. In Rust, it "
"might be written like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:269
msgid ""
"The function has been turned into a method to make it more convenient to "
"use. However, if you want to look for the Rust equivalent of "
"`lib_name_do_something`, you have no way to do so."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:273
msgid ""
"To get around this limitation, we just add `#[doc(alias = "
"\"lib_name_do_something\")]` on the `do_something` method and then it's all "
"good! Users can now look for `lib_name_do_something` in our crate directly "
"and find `Obj::do_something`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:278
msgid "`test(attr(...))`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:280
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute allows you to add arbitrary attributes to "
"all your doctests. For example, if you want your doctests to fail if they "
"have dead code, you could add this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:287
msgid "// but allow `dead_code` for this module\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:291
msgid ""
"`test(attr(..))` attributes are appended to the parent module's, they do not "
"replace the current list of attributes. In the previous example, both "
"attributes would be present:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:295
msgid "// For every doctest in `my_mod`\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:297
msgid "// from the crate-root\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:298
msgid "// from `my_mod`\n"
msgstr ""
